# Build output
build/
# Side-by-side build trees, e.g. build-nocuda/ for the --no-cuda configuration that
# CI's first job uses.  This existed and was NOT covered by the rule above, so a
# `git add -A` would have committed a whole second build tree.  The suffix form is
# deliberate: `build/` alone is what let that happen.
build-*/
build*/
out/
*.obj
*.pdb
*.ilk
*.exp
*.lib

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vendored dependencies -- NOT part of rdither, and enormous
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FFmpeg-master/ is 10,772 files and 88 MB of someone else's project, unpacked
# alongside the source for reference.  OpenCL-SDK-* is the Khronos SDK, which
# CMakeLists.txt auto-detects beside the tree.  Both are third-party, both are
# re-obtainable from their own projects, and committing either would bury this
# repository's own history under theirs.
#
# The README says where each comes from and that the build expects them beside
# the source rather than inside it.
/FFmpeg-master/
/OpenCL-SDK-*/

# claimlint.py is somebody else's tool, kept in the working tree and NOT tracked.
# It arrived from outside this repository's history, it is not part of the build or
# the suite, and nothing in .github\workflows\ci.yml invokes it.  Tracking it would
# put a 33 kB script of unknown provenance into this project's history and imply the
# project depends on it, which it does not.
#
# .cache/ is that tool's name index: 9.5 MB of derived data rebuilt from the tree on
# demand, which is exactly what a cache is for and exactly what should not be committed.
#
# .claimlint-baseline.json would be the record of findings accepted as benign, so that
# exit 0 keeps meaning "nothing new" on a later run.  It does not exist yet because no
# baseline has been accepted; if one is, it belongs here too.
claimlint.py
/.cache/
/.claimlint-baseline.json

# Probe binaries.  tools\probe_swscale_matrix.cpp is tracked; its .exe is not,
# because a 220 KB binary in a source tree is the kind of thing that gets
# committed by accident and then conflicts on every branch.  Rebuild it from
# the .cpp.  The scripts under tools\ are tracked -- they are the record of how
# the measurements were taken, which is the part worth keeping.
*.exe

# Rendered clips and scratch frames
*.mkv
*.mp4
*.nut
n.mkv
o.mkv

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test fixtures
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# tests/ splits into three kinds of file, and only one of them belongs in git.
#
#   INPUTS      in_*.png, noisy.png, t_*.png, c*.png, g*.png  -- the images
#               verify.ps1 builds its cases from.  Tracked, because the test
#               suite is the thing worth reproducing.
#
#   OUTPUTS     *_ref.png, *_cpu.png, *_cuda.png, *_cpu_disk.png -- regenerated
#               by every verify.ps1 run and by probe-opencl-exact.ps1.  180
#               files, ~5 MB, and they are stale the moment anything changes.
#               Tracked, they turn every measurement into a spurious diff.
#
#   LEFTOVERS   fhd.png, fhd_photo.png, big.png, smooth.png -- 23 MB of large
#               images that no test, script or source file references at all
#               (checked: zero hits across verify.ps1, tools\*.ps1 and src\).
#               Not committed, and regenerable from the same ImageMagick calls
#               the fixtures use.
*_ref.png
*_cpu.png
*_cuda.png
*_cpu_disk.png
tests/fhd.png
tests/fhd_photo.png
tests/big.png
tests/smooth.png

# Run transcripts from this tree: gate and build logs.  They are evidence ABOUT a
# machine, not about the code -- a gate log is a claim about what passed on one
# GTX 1650 SUPER on one day, and committing ten of them invites a diff that means
# nothing.  The same reasoning as artifacts/ below, which is why that directory is
# ignored rather than tracked.
#
# This rule was MISSING, and its absence is how `git add -A` would have committed
# gate.log, gate2.log, ... gate_slow.log, gate_now.log, gate_after_fixes.log,
# gate_cuda.log, build_cuda.log and build_cuda2.log -- every one of them written by
# an agent session, none of them reviewed.  A .gitignore entry is cheaper than
# remembering which of thirty-nine untracked paths are yours.
gate*.log
build_*.log

# Editor and OS noise
.vscode/
.vs/
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store
*.swp

# Hi-res source photographs for the README showcase.  Regenerate from the
# Wikimedia Commons URLs recorded in docs/examples/SOURCES.md; the dithered
# outputs in docs/examples/ are what gets committed.
*.orig.jpg

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# artifacts/ -- scratch, and one thing that is deliberately NOT portable
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything here is either regenerated on demand or machine-specific:
#
#   gate_*.log, cpubudget*.log, build_*.log   run transcripts, overwritten each run
#   geom_oracle.ps1, fuse_oracle.ps1          the SHORT oracles, written during the
#                                              session that needed them.  Untracked, so
#                                              they are not a documented interface --
#                                              the gate does not invoke them.
#   colors32_findings.md                      the measurement write-up for the
#                                              --colors 32 investigation
#   video_pixels_baseline.txt                THE ONE THAT MATTERS, and the reason this
#                                              entry exists at all.
#
# That baseline is a SHA256 per engine per colour count, captured on the machine that
# captured it.  Committing it would make every OTHER machine's first run fail for a
# reason that has nothing to do with its own code: a different GPU produces a different
# hash legitimately, and the probe would report DIFFER against a baseline recorded on
# somebody else's silicon.  A check that is red on arrival teaches people to ignore it.
#
# So the PROBE is tracked (tools\probe-video-pixels.ps1) and the baseline is not.  Each
# machine writes its own with -Write BEFORE a risky change and compares after.  It is
# evidence about that machine, which is the only kind of evidence a hash can be.
#
# The whole directory was previously untracked-but-not-ignored, which is the state
# `git add -A` exists to turn into an accident.
artifacts/

# Per-plan scratch for the SDD/executing-plans skills: ledger, briefs, review
# packages. Git history is the record; these are working state.
.superpowers/
